# L §5.18 Explanation order and E §10: rules print in canonical order (§2.4) and each cause prints once, a cause met
# again referenced by its line. Rules are evaluated beaters first (z, then a), so big is first evaluated under z's
# when but first printed under a: it prints in full there and as a reference under z. f = r = 2 (lim 1 > r 2 is
# false, so z is not applicable and a wins). (Was: REFCORE-NOT-YET ... KeyError, the printer referencing a cause
# not yet printed; the household's explain_room crash had this signature.)
=== m.tally
model m
  tally 2
  on 2026-10-18
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "synthetic"
unit PLN: currency minor 0.01

input lim: int
input r: int

def big: bool = lim > r

def f: int
  rule a = r when not big
  rule z beats a = lim when big

ask e: explain f

version v1
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  lim = 1
  r = 2
=== expect
e: 2
  [1] f = 2
      rule a: won
        [2] big = false
            rule base: won
              [3] row v1:lim = 1
                  version v1, source s, "synthetic", governs always, known 2026-01-01
              [4] row v1:r = 2
                  version v1, source s, "synthetic", governs always, known 2026-01-01
        (v1:r: see [4])
      rule z: not applicable
        (big: see [2])
  cut 2026-10-18
